@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+# Explicit Workspace authorization for the Mastermind COO connection
+
+This source extension composes the existing Codex OAuth, credential-store and HTTP owners. It is an opt-in API-principal capability. It does not attest a T3 thread, provider turn, tool invocation or native organizational identity.
+
+The setting is valid only for the local `mastermind_coo` server at `https://mcp.mastermind-x.com/mcp/coo`, with OAuth authentication, an explicitly registered public client, the resource `https://mcp.mastermind-x.com/mcp`, and exactly these configured scopes:
+
+```toml
+scopes = ["mastermind.executive.coo.act", "mastermind.executive.read"]
+oauth_resource = "https://mcp.mastermind-x.com/mcp"
+```
+
+The existing `oauth` client configuration may additionally select the original Mastermind descriptor:
+
+```toml
+[mcp_servers.mastermind_coo.oauth.workspace]
+schema = "mastermind.hermes_workspace_oauth.v1"
+resource = "https://mcp.mastermind-x.com/workspace/read"
+identity_url = "https://mcp.mastermind-x.com/workspace/native-draft-identity/current"
+scope = "mastermind.workspace.read"
+principal_scope = "<exact 64 lower-case hexadecimal characters from the enrolled source>"
+```
+
+The placeholder is deliberately invalid. This example supplies neither enrollment nor consent. The source/authorization owner must provide the actual client and principal-scope binding. Other resources, endpoints, transport aliases, remote execution environments, static/env bearer overrides, generic header helpers, confidential clients and alternative auth modes are refused for this opt-in. Omitting `oauth.workspace` preserves the ordinary single-resource path and encoding.
+
+## Consent and credential custody
+
+The original explicit `codex mcp login mastermind_coo` browser flow performs two sequential authorization-code/PKCE consents. Both use the configured public client and their independently requested resource and scope. The first grant is stored under the original primary identity. The second is stored using the same credential-store implementation under `mastermind_coo_workspace` and the fixed Workspace resource URL. There is no new store format, credential service or token relay.
+
+Cancellation or refusal during the second consent does not erase the completed first grant or claim paired readiness. Starting an MCP connection never starts a login. The app-server's one-URL login RPC, silent login and pasted-callback flow refuse the opt-in before creating a login flow. The app-server also refuses before resolving a header helper or performing scope discovery. The CLI cannot retry paired login with empty scopes. A deployment must retain this explicit-browser limitation until its actual UI can represent both consents.
+
+An existing primary bearer alone is insufficient. The ordinary credential status projection reports `Unknown` for the pair; the actual initialized connection owns usable readiness. Removing either required credential makes the next protected request fail closed. Local credential removal is distinct from authorization-server revocation.
+
+## Original request ownership
+
+The existing native MCP factory validates the full selected configuration. Its original connection fingerprint includes the selected descriptor, OAuth configuration, scopes and resource, so a source change rebuilds the connection. The transport retains the selected home and each concrete credential backend. Both resources use the original coordinated refresh transaction. The credential store checks retained home custody before reads and mutations, including refresh persistence and retirement.
+
+Before an original protected GET, POST or DELETE, the same captured HTTP capability obtains a bounded Workspace identity proof. Its response must be exactly `mastermind.native_draft_identity.v1`, the selected `principal_scope`, and a future `expires_at_ms`, within 1,024 bytes. The proof endpoint receives the Workspace bearer only. The primary endpoint receives its original Executive bearer plus `x-mastermind-workspace-authorization`. Neither companion header nor bearer goes to OAuth metadata/token endpoints, other URLs, or redirects.
+
+The client rereads current credentials before and after proof acquisition and after the original HTTP response. For streaming responses, that final check brackets response headers; the original HTTP/MCP stream owner continues to own subsequent bytes and cancellation. This transport does not replace the application's own current-source/result validation.
+
+No new retry is added. The pair suppresses RMCP's reactive 401 refresh-and-resubmit behavior: a rejected original request returns a closed grant rejection. A lost original response or a failed post-send currentness check returns `WORKSPACE_OAUTH_ORIGINAL_OUTCOME_UNCERTAIN`, requiring reconciliation of the original application operation. Only an explicit prewrite `RequestNotSent` from the original HTTP owner retains that classification. Cancellation drops the original proof/HTTP future; already-owned refresh persistence retains its incumbent cancellation behavior.
+
+## Qualification and installation boundary
+
+The focused tests use the actual config parser, OAuth manager, PKCE callback, pinned credential stores, refresh transactions and MCP handshake with a disposable supplied HTTP capability. They use no real account, installed profile, browser consent, model or native session. The canonical config schema is regenerated by the existing `codex-config-schema` package.
+
+This source must be reviewed and built for the intended native Codex distribution, then installed through its existing release owner before T3 can use it. Actual Executive and Workspace enrollment/consent remain separate requirements. This feature supplies an ordinary authenticated API avenue; native T3 origin-attester enrollment remains a separate, unavailable capability.
